@image1 (render exactly as the reference — no redesign of face, hair, or outfit) Setting: A modern high-rise interior inside a Wall Street skyscraper in Lower Manhattan's Financial District at night. A massive floor-to-ceiling window faces the glittering nighttime skyline — the illuminated glass and stone towers of the Financial District, One World Trade Center rising in the distance with its glowing spire, thousands of office lights, red aviation beacons on rooftops, the dark ribbon of the East River with the lit cables of the Brooklyn Bridge, light trails of traffic in the narrow canyon streets far below. The room is dim, lit only by the cold blue-and-amber glow of the city outside and subtle warm interior accent lighting. Visual style: Cinematic photorealistic quality, slow-motion glass shattering VFX (stylized for beauty, not danger or gore), high-contrast night lighting, rich particle effects, strictly no blood or realistic injury imagery. 

Duration: 15s | Aspect ratio: 16:9 | Resolution: 4K | Frame rate: 24fps

Shot-by-shot script (camera changes every ~2 seconds): 0–2s — Quiet night (eye-level medium shot): Eye-level shot. She stands calmly before the huge floor-to-ceiling window, the glow of the night city washing over her, relaxed expression as she gazes out at the glittering Wall Street skyline. Everything is serene and beautiful. 2–3s — Tremor warning (frontal reaction close-up): Cut to frontal close-up. A faint vibration rises through the floor beneath her feet, the hanging curtains beside her sway gently. Her brow furrows slightly, expression shifting from relaxed to alert. 3–4s — Violent shaking (handheld shaky cam, audio anchor): A violent tremor strikes with a deep rumbling roar — handheld camera shakes hard. She reaches out in panic to brace against the wall, fine cracks begin spreading across the window glass, city lights refracting through them. 4–5.5s — Glass fracture ignition (extreme slow-motion close-up): Cut to extreme slow motion. Glass cracks spread with stylized beauty, web-like across the entire floor-to-ceiling window, glowing veins of neon city light running through every fracture line. She turns and backs away in fright, long hair whipping with the motion. 5.5–7s — Shards in flight (360° orbiting slow-motion shot): Camera orbits her in 360° slow motion as the massive window explodes into millions of crystalline shards. Each fragment catches the neon skyline — refracting blue, amber, and red city lights like scattered sapphires and embers — suspended and drifting through the dark air like a diamond rain. She raises her hand in shock to shield her face. 7–9s — Suspended shards, frozen gaze (low-angle close-up): Low-angle close-up: she stares in awe through her fingers at the beautiful and dangerous storm of shards before her. Night city light pierces the floating glass, scattering countless shifting specks of colored light across her face, hair drifting in the air current. 9–11s — Shards passing by (slow-motion lateral tracking shot): Lateral tracking shot captures several glass shards gliding past her in slow motion, miraculously never touching her (stylized). She instinctively closes her eyes and turns aside, the air current tugging at her clothes. 11–12.5s — Dust settles (high-angle wide shot): Camera pulls up and away, high-angle wide. Glass fragments drift down like snowfall, covering the floor. The night skyline pours in unobstructed through the broken window frame, casting dappled cold light and long shadows across the room. She stands shaken amid the shards. 12.5–14s — Catching her breath (slow frontal push-in): Slow push-in on her face. She breathes heavily, looking at the shattered window and the glittering shards covering the floor, her eyes shifting from fear to a complex expression of relief. 14–15s — FREEZE FRAME (finale): Camera pulls back to wide. She stands before the broken window with the vast glowing night panorama of the Wall Street Financial District behind her — One World Trade Center's spire shining on the horizon — the shattered glass across the floor sparkling like a field of stars reflecting the skyline — the frame freezes on this moment, then fades to black. Camera work: eye-level medium → frontal reaction close-up → handheld shake → extreme slow-motion close-up → 360° orbit slow motion → low-angle close-up → slow-motion lateral tracking → high-angle wide → slow frontal push-in → freeze frame. Camera changes every 1.5–2 seconds; after the quake hits, pacing deliberately slows to emphasize slow-motion beauty. VFX emphasis: curtain sway as tremor warning, web-like crack propagation glowing with neon light, shards refracting multicolored city lights, diamond-rain suspension of fragments, air currents moving hair and clothing, dappled night light through the broken frame, floor of glass sparkling like a starfield. Sound design: 0–2s calm nighttime interior ambience with distant city hum → 2–3s faint rumbling crescendo → 3–4s violent quake roar (audio anchor) → 4–9s crystalline glass-shatter sounds stretched and elongated (slow-motion audio treatment) → 9–12s rushing wind and airflow → 12–15s fading back to calm: her breathing and quiet night ambience. No dialogue, no voice lines — only ambient sound, effects, and breathing. Negative prompts: no blood, cuts, or realistic injury imagery; glass shards must never appear lethal or harmful; no character inconsistency between shots; no repeating the same camera angle consecutively; no defocus or blur on the freeze frame; no speech, no dialogue, no lip-sync.
